WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.620
Your body dismantles roughly 250 grams of protein daily.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.160
Why spend ATP to destroy what the cell already built?

3
00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:15.280
Follow one protein through a recycling center, where selection matters more than

4
00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:16.280
demolition.

5
00:00:16.933 --> 00:00:19.513
Proteins have different expiration dates.

6
00:00:20.673 --> 00:00:23.713
Some last minutes; others last months or a lifetime.

7
00:00:25.193 --> 00:00:28.433
Turnover removes damaged proteins and changes signals.

8
00:00:29.833 --> 00:00:32.033
Yet peptide-bond hydrolysis is downhill.

9
00:00:33.413 --> 00:00:34.513
What is ATP buying?

10
00:00:35.567 --> 00:00:37.787
Ubiquitin is the address label.

11
00:00:39.247 --> 00:00:41.387
E1 spends ATP to activate it.

12
00:00:42.527 --> 00:00:48.427
E2 carries it. An E3 ligase recognizes the target and applies the tag.

13
00:00:50.067 --> 00:00:52.047
Hundreds of E3s create selectivity.

14
00:00:53.287 --> 00:00:57.627
A classic K48 chain sends the protein toward the proteasome.

15
00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:00.440
Predict where energy goes.

16
00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:05.580
Does ATP power peptide-bond cutting, or pay for something else?

17
00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:09.940
Hold your answer as the tagged protein reaches the machine.

18
00:01:15.533 --> 00:01:23.093
Not to cutting. The proteasome's regulatory cap recognizes the tag, then uses ATP to

19
00:01:23.093 --> 00:01:25.093
unfold and thread the protein.

20
00:01:26.353 --> 00:01:29.423
The core cuts it into short peptides without ATP.

21
00:01:29.423 --> 00:01:32.653
Deubiquitinases recover the tag.

22
00:01:33.893 --> 00:01:38.433
Energy buys choice and controlled handling, not favorable bond hydrolysis.

23
00:01:39.467 --> 00:01:43.167
Peptidases release amino acids into a shared pool.

24
00:01:44.407 --> 00:01:48.027
In an adult at nitrogen balance, most return to protein.

25
00:01:49.247 --> 00:01:50.447
The surplus is burned.

26
00:01:51.707 --> 00:01:57.447
The cell selects proteins, reuses most products, then separates surplus amino acids

27
00:01:57.447 --> 00:01:58.447
by atom.

28
00:01:59.067 --> 00:02:04.707
In this simplest adult human picture, each burned amino acid poses two problems.

29
00:02:06.167 --> 00:02:09.267
Nitrogen can be toxic; carbon remains valuable.

30
00:02:10.747 --> 00:02:15.587
Nitrogen passes onto glutamate and often through alanine or glutamine in blood.

31
00:02:16.767 --> 00:02:20.087
The carbon skeleton moves toward central metabolism.

32
00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:24.760
In the liver, most waste nitrogen is packaged as urea.

33
00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:30.440
One urea nitrogen comes from ammonium; the other comes from aspartate.

34
00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:35.100
The cycle spends four high-energy phosphate bonds per urea.

35
00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:39.760
It converts a dangerous load into an excretable product.

36
00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:44.430
Carbon skeletons enter through seven metabolic doors.

37
00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:49.930
Five can supply net glucose because they can become oxaloacetate.

38
00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:55.510
The acetyl-CoA and acetoacetyl-CoA doors cannot.

39
00:02:56.850 --> 00:02:59.450
Only leucine and lysine are purely ketogenic.

40
00:03:00.533 --> 00:03:03.293
Now the recycling center's logic is visible.

41
00:03:04.593 --> 00:03:07.493
Select the protein before dismantling it.

42
00:03:08.313 --> 00:03:09.873
Reuse most amino acids.

43
00:03:11.153 --> 00:03:15.653
When surplus is burned, cage its nitrogen and keep its carbon in metabolism.

44
00:03:17.193 --> 00:03:21.753
Choose what dies, recycle most, then split surplus amino acids by atom.
